# Deploying {{ .Container }} to Kubernetes

This folder holds plain Kubernetes manifests for {{ .Container }}, built with
kustomize. C4 Factory ships them through an `argoCD` DeployTarget: at each
release, Argo CD syncs this folder at the release's tag, with the release's
image. To ship them yourself, point Argo CD, Flux or a pipeline at
`deploy/k8s`, or apply it by hand as below. Run the commands from the
repository's root.

## 1. Build the image

{{ if $dockerfile -}}
The `Dockerfile` at the root builds the image:
{{- else -}}
This repository brings its own `Dockerfile`; the manifests expect the app to
listen on port {{ $port }} and answer `GET {{ $probe }}`. Build it with:
{{- end }}

```sh
docker build -t {{ .Container }}:1.0.0 .
```

Each `v*` tag's release builds it too, for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`
(`.gitea/workflows/image.yaml`), and the factory pushes it to the Git host's
container registry. Nothing emulates the other platform, so the `Dockerfile`
`RUN`s only in stages `FROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM` and its last stage only
`COPY`s; keep it so, or the release fails.

To push one yourself where your cluster can pull it from, for example:

```sh
docker tag {{ .Container }}:1.0.0 registry.example.com/{{ .Container }}:1.0.0
docker push registry.example.com/{{ .Container }}:1.0.0
```

## 2. Set the image

`{{ $workload }}` runs the placeholder image `{{ .Container }}:dev`. Point it at
the image you pushed; this writes an `images` entry into `kustomization.yaml`:

```sh
cd deploy/k8s
kustomize edit set image {{ .Container }}=registry.example.com/{{ .Container }}:1.0.0
cd -
```

## 3. Preview

```sh
kustomize build deploy/k8s
```

`kubectl kustomize deploy/k8s` prints the same without kustomize installed.

## 4. Apply
{{- if eq .Framework "python-fastapi" }}

The app verifies a bearer token on every route but its probes, and refuses to
start without `OIDC_ISSUER` and `OIDC_AUDIENCE`. It reads both from ConfigMap
`{{ .Container }}-auth` in `auth.yaml`, which ships placeholders: set
`OIDC_ISSUER` to your issuer's URL (the app reads its keys from
`$OIDC_ISSUER/.well-known/openid-configuration`) and `OIDC_AUDIENCE` to the
`aud` its tokens carry. Until you do, the app starts and every route but the
probes answers 503.
{{- end }}
{{- if has .Connectors "postgres" }}

The database and the app read the Postgres password from Secret
`{{ .Container }}-postgres`, key `password`. Make it once, before the first
apply; it is never written in this repository:

```sh
kubectl create secret generic {{ .Container }}-postgres --from-literal=password="$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
```
{{- end }}

{{- if $job }}

```sh
kubectl apply -k deploy/k8s
kubectl wait --for=condition=complete job/{{ .Container }}
kubectl logs job/{{ .Container }}
```

Add `-n <namespace>` to each, or set `namespace:` in `kustomization.yaml`. A
Job's template is immutable, so to run it again (a new image, say) delete it
and apply again; the other objects are unchanged, so apply leaves them alone:

```sh
kubectl delete job/{{ .Container }}
kubectl apply -k deploy/k8s
```
{{- else }}

```sh
kubectl apply -k deploy/k8s
kubectl rollout status deployment/{{ .Container }}
```

Add `-n <namespace>` to both, or set `namespace:` in `kustomization.yaml`.
{{- end }}

## What is here

- `kustomization.yaml`: lists the files below and the image to run.
{{- if $job }}
- `job.yaml`: a Job that runs container `app` once to completion
  (`restartPolicy: Never`), as a non-root user with no privilege escalation.
  Its annotation `argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Force=true,Replace=true`
  makes Argo CD delete and recreate the Job at each release instead of
  patching it (`Replace=true` alone is an update, which the immutable
  template refuses), so each release runs it again; the finished Job and its pod stay, with their logs, until the
  next release.
{{- else }}
- `deployment.yaml`: one replica of container `app`, as a non-root user with
  no privilege escalation.
{{- end }}
{{- if $service }}
- `service.yaml`: Service `{{ .Container }}` on port 80, forwarding to the
  container's port `http`.
{{- end }}
{{- if eq .Framework "python-fastapi" }}
- `auth.yaml`: ConfigMap `{{ .Container }}-auth` with `OIDC_ISSUER` and
  `OIDC_AUDIENCE`, the token issuer and audience the app verifies against,
  which the {{ if $job }}Job{{ else }}Deployment{{ end }} loads through `envFrom`.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Compute }}
- `compute.yaml`: the CPU and memory requests and limits of container `app`,
  written by the Compute layer ({{ .Compute }}). A patch, listed under `patches`
  in `kustomization.yaml`; change the sizes there.
{{- end }}
{{- range .Connectors }}
{{- if eq . "postgres" }}
- `connector-postgres.yaml`: the database, written by the postgres Connector:
  StatefulSet and Service `{{ $.Container }}-postgres` (one Postgres with its
  own volume) and ConfigMap `{{ $.Container }}-postgres` with `DATABASE_URL`,
  which the {{ if $job }}Job{{ else }}Deployment{{ end }} loads through `envFrom`. Postgres and the app read the
  password from Secret `{{ $.Container }}-postgres`.
{{- else }}
- `connector-{{ . }}.yaml`: ConfigMap `{{ $.Container }}-{{ . }}`, the
  connection settings for {{ . }}, written by its Connector. The
  {{ if $job }}Job{{ else }}Deployment{{ end }} loads it into the container's
  environment through `envFrom`; read the file
  for what to point at what you provision. Keep passwords in a Secret, never
  in the ConfigMap.
{{- end }}
{{- end }}

## Port and probes

{{ if $job -}}
The container runs to completion, so it has no port and no probes. Kubernetes
retries it up to 3 times (`backoffLimit`) when it fails; `kubectl describe
job/{{ .Container }}` and the pod's logs say why.
{{- else if $readyz -}}
The container listens on port {{ $port }}, named `http`. The readiness probe
asks `GET /readyz`, which runs `SELECT 1` on the database: while the database
doesn't answer, the pod leaves Service `{{ .Container }}` and gets no traffic,
and it comes back when the database does. The liveness probe asks
`GET {{ $probe }}`, which doesn't touch the database, and restarts the container
when it stops answering (after 10 seconds' grace at start), so a database
outage never restarts the app.
{{- else if $http -}}
The container listens on port {{ $port }}, named `http`. Kubernetes asks
`GET {{ $probe }}` on it: the readiness probe holds traffic until it answers, and
the liveness probe restarts the container when it stops answering (after 10
seconds' grace at start).
{{- else -}}
The container serves nothing, so it has no port and no probes. Kubernetes
restarts it when it exits; it stops cleanly on the SIGTERM a pod gets when it
is stopped.
{{- end }}
